\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f2\fs20 
\par 
\par To take advantage of the Devasys functions in BCB you need to do two things, one modify the header file supplied by Devasys and two, create a new lib file to access the usbi2cio.dll.
\par 
\par I have included a modified header file and new lib file in this zip (usbi2cio_bcb_v5.h & bcb_usbi2cio.lib) for those of you that would like to get started right away.  Also included is a BCB Demo Project in \'93Demo.zip\'94.
\par 
\par If you would like more information on using VC++ dll\rquote s and lib\rquote s with BCB take a look at "BCBDEV.COM Using Visual C++ DLLs in a C++Builder Project.pdf" which was included in this zip or check out Borland\rquote 
s Developers Network at http://bdn.borland.com/.
\par 
\par OK, now this is going to be a very net how-to.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\f2\fs20\cf1 Steps for modifying usbi2cio.h file }{\f2\fs20\cf1 
\par }{\b\f2\fs20\cf1 
\par }{\f2\fs20\cf1 I have found the only real changes that need to be made are to the header file is that function calls have to be defined as external "C" & adding __declspec(dllimport) to the beginning of each function line.
\par }{\b\f2\fs20\cf1 
\par }{\f2\fs20\cf1 You will find comments in the modified header file showing where changes were made to the Devasys header.
\par 
\par If you want to change the function names that you use in your program to something else then here is the place to change it. Remember if you do you
 will also have to create a .def file to translate these new names into the ones that are in the DLL. Take a look }{\f2\fs20 at "BCBDEV.COM Using Visual C++ DLLs in a C++Builder Project.pdf" for more information.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\f2\fs20 Steps for using the Visual C++ Dll/lib provided 
\par }{\f2\fs20 
\par You will find a number of exe files in the Borland C++ Builder \\
bin subdirectory that you will need to use for the next few steps.  You might find it easier to create a new working subdirectory and copy these exe files along with the original usbi2cio.dll file into it.
\par 
\par IMPLIB.EXE
\par TLIB.EXE
\par COFF2OMF.EXE
\par TDUMP.EXE
\par IMPDEF.EXE
\par 
\par You are going to be working from a DOS command line so you might want to make the name of your new subdirectory short.
\par 
\par So here we go:
\par 
